{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589351135,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc5995001PeRPwa8b61hPgX","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc4d1c001zMK4MiRuVbyAcf","type":"tool","id":"call_d5371da9b35042579d9def67","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaults | `specs/**` | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` |\n187: | SKILLED | `.skilled/**` | `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n188: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/**` | `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n189: | SKILLED | `.skilled/agents/**` | `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n190: | SKILLED | `.skilled/commands/**` | `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n191: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.cjs`, `.mjs` | `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n192: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md` |\n193: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md` |\n194: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md` |\n195: | SKILLED | `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.mts`, `.cts` | `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n196: | SKILLED | `.py` | `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n197: | SKILLED | `.sh`, `.bash` | `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n198: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md` |\n199: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md` |\n200: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md` |\n201: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md` |\n202: | SKILLED | `.json`, `.jsonc`, `.yaml`, `.yml` | `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n203: | sk-code-ci | `.github/workflows/**` | `../sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md, lines 1-203\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code-skilled\n3: description: \"sk-code SKILLED surface: system-code evidence (TypeScript/Python/shell/Rust/config standards, language-agnostic patterns, hooks, alignment verification, and skill/agent/command/MCP authoring checklists) plus shared implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.1.18.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8:   packetKind: surface\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence\n12: \n13: **Domain evidence** and shared workflow doctrine for OpenCode system code (the `.skilled/` tree: skills, agents, commands, plugins, MCP servers, config, changelogs, and runtime bridge wiring). This surface owns the implement -> debug -> verify phases through the workflow references below, then slices evidence by the detected language so a TypeScript task never pulls the Python/shell/config guides.\n14: \n15: Detection has two steps:\n16: \n17: - First, select the Skilled surface for work under `.skilled/`. This includes `SKILL.md`, descriptors, commands, agents, plugins, MCP servers, assets, scripts and changelogs.\n18: - Then use file extensions and local markers to select the language trio:\n19:   - `.cjs`/`.mjs`/`.js` -> JavaScript\n20:   - `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.mts`/`.d.ts` -> TypeScript\n21:   - `.py` plus `argparse` -> Python\n22:   - `.sh`/`.bash` -> shell\n23:   - `.rs` -> Rust\n24:   - `.json`/`.jsonc`/`.yaml`/`.yml` plus `graph-metadata` or `spec-folder` -> config\n25: \n26: For Rust, when no `.rs` file is present, local `Cargo.toml`/`Cargo.lock` markers select it after the Skilled surface is established.\n27: napi-rs and wasm-bindgen vocabulary are additional intent signals, not cross-project surface detectors.\n28: \n29: ## 1. WHEN THE HUB BUNDLES THIS\n30: \n31: - The task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, or descriptor/config.\n32: - The active workflow phase needs a language standard, a language-agnostic organization pattern, a hook contract, an alignment-verification procedure, or an authoring checklist.\n33: - This surface owns edits, tests, and verification through the workflow references; hand off formal findings-first review to `sk-code-review` and author-side quality gates to `sk-code-quality`.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n38: \n39: The hub bundles this surface when the task touches `.skilled/` system code — a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config — and a language or authoring signal then selects the leaf. The first leaves it loads are the shared tier under `references/shared/` and `references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md`, because every language standard builds on them.\n40: \n41: The parent hub `RESOURCE_MAP` carries this map re-prefixed with `sk-code-skilled/` plus the parent-owned universal tier, and the router-sync guard (`assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs`, leg 2) checks that equality. A new doc under `references/` or `assets/` needs a `RESOURCE_MAP` entry here, or leg 1b fails.\n42: \n43: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 3. REFERENCE MAP\n48: \n49: Language standards — after `.skilled/` selects this surface, load the exact split resources for the detected language:\n50: - TypeScript:\n51:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md`\n52:   - `references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md`\n53:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md`\n54:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md`\n55:   - `references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md`\n56:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md`\n57:   - `references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md`\n58: - Python — `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md`, `references/python/quick-reference.md`\n59: - Shell:\n60:   - `references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md`\n61:   - `references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md`\n62:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md`\n63:   - `references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md`\n64:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md`\n65:   - `references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md`\n66: - Rust:\n67:   - Style guide:\n68:     - `references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md`\n69:     - `references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md`\n70:     - `references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md`\n71:     - `references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md`\n72:     - `references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md`\n73:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md`\n74:     - `references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md`\n75:   - Quality standards:\n76:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md`\n77:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md`\n78:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md`\n79:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md`\n80:     - `references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md`\n81:   - Quick reference:\n82:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md`\n83:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md`\n84:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md`\n85:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md`\n86:     - `references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md`\n87: - Config (JSON/JSONC/YAML descriptors and route assets) — `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md`, `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`\n88: - JavaScript (CommonJS/ESM plugins) — `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md`, `references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md`, `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`\n89: \n90: Language-agnostic shared tier (`references/shared/`, always kept within OpenCode regardless of language):\n91: - `references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`, `references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md`, `references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md`\n92: - `hooks.md` — runtime hook entrypoints, checked-in Claude wiring, OpenCode plugin-bridge delivery, and wrapper reachability; defer to that file for current hook infrastructure instead of duplicating it here\n93: - `alignment-verification-automation.md` — the alignment-drift verifier\n94: - `workflow-guardrails.md`: OpenCode-only implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and runtime build traps, applied on top of the shared implement and verify workflow\n95: \n96: Authoring and validation assets:\n97: \n98: - Checklists:\n99:   - `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`\n100:   - `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`\n101:   - `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md`\n102:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md`\n103:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md`\n104:   - `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md`\n105:   - `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`\n106:   - `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md`\n107:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md`\n108:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md`\n109:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md`\n110:   - `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md`\n111:   - `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md`\n112:   - `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md`\n113:   - `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md`\n114:   - `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`\n115: - Scripts:\n116:   - `assets/scripts/README.md`\n117: - References:\n118:   - `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`\n119:   - `references/shared/hooks.md`\n120: \n121: Workflow: `references/workflow-implement.md`, `references/workflow-debug.md` and `references/workflow-verify.md` link to the hub's shared implement -> debug -> verify doctrine in `../../shared/references/`; the split shared, language, hook and alignment resources above apply on top of it.\n122: \n123: ---\n124: \n125: ## 4. SURFACE STANDARDS (the non-negotiables)\n126: \n127: - **Plugins never write to the TUI.** OpenCode plugins must not print to the process stdout/stderr (no overlay on the chat input); user/agent-visible output goes through system-context injection, tools, or append-only log files; DEBUG-gated stderr is allowed only behind an env flag. See `references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md` and the plugin exemption tier.\n128: - **Descriptors are load-bearing.** `graph-metadata.json` / `description.json` shape drives discovery; validate JSON/JSONC against `references/config/quality-standards.md`.\n129: - **Alignment drift is a verification gate.** Before any completion claim, re-run the four live sk-code drift guards on a system-code change through the single entry point `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh`.\n130:   - `assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` checks language integrity, plus dead RESOURCE_MAP routes with `--check-router`.\n131:   - `assets/scripts/verify_stack_folders.py` checks that language reference folders resolve.\n132:   - `assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs` is the router-sync guard. It restores the four checks of the `sk-code-router-sync.vitest.ts` suite, which was deleted, and the wrapper runs every leg of it: 1a, 1b, 2, 3 and 4.\n133:     - Check (1): router paths exist on disk, every routable doc is routed, and every full path the prose maps name is routed.\n134:     - Check (2): the parent RESOURCE_MAP equals the union of the surface children's maps plus the parent tier.\n135:     - Check (3): compiled route-gold destinations, `leaf-manifest.json` and this RESOURCE_MAP agree through `qualifiedIdToLeaf`.\n136:     - Check (4): every playbook routing scenario's `expected_resource` is emitted by the router.\n137:   - The dead-path part of check (1) is also checked by `--check-router`. Leg 1b, the orphan-doc walk, fails on any reference or asset doc that no router names. It allowlists only the three shared workflow docs, which each surface reaches through a symlink.\n138:   - `.github/workflows/routing-registry-drift.yml` covers the compiled side of (3) and (4) in CI only, with the admission step warn-only.\n139:   - The documentation claim guard (`assets/scripts/verify-doc-claims.cjs`) reads the sk-code prose the other guards skip, from the hub folder on disk.\n140:     It checks that path references resolve and that no retired packet name remains.\n141:     It also checks that every surface count matches the surface modes in `mode-registry.json`, and that every file the `ROUTER.md` load-tier prose says loads on every route is in `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`.\n142:     Lines that keep a legacy name on purpose sit in its allowlist with a reason. See `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`.\n143:   The wrapper scans repository content only: the files git tracks, so ignored trees, sibling worktrees and vendored clones stay out of a gate they can never pass. It exits 0 on a clean tree, so a completion claim requires wrapper rc 0 rather than a delta against a backlog.\n144: - **Rust preserves the TypeScript contract.** Rust napi-rs, WASM/WASI, and sidecar modules are compatibility implementations, not independent behavior authorities. JS-visible bytes, six-decimal numeric behavior, comparator tie-breaks, deterministic IDs, collection order, DTOs, and error shapes must remain identical to the TypeScript oracle.\n145: - **Touched-language set, not one-per-task.** Most `.skilled/` tasks touch a single language. Keep that slice tight and use the shared tier for cross-language rules.\n146: \n147: An interop task that spans a language pair, such as a napi-rs / WASM / sidecar Rust module held to its TypeScript oracle, legitimately touches both languages.\n148: The router selects the languages the task actually touches and loads both trios plus the shared tier.\n149: You cannot hold Rust byte-identical to TypeScript without seeing both standards.\n150: \n151: ---\n152: \n153: ## 5. SUPPORTING ASSETS\n154: \n155: Component authoring (`assets/checklists/`): `skill-authoring.md`, `agent-authoring.md`, `command-authoring.md`, `mcp-server-authoring.md`\n156: \n157: Language quality gates (`assets/checklists/`): `universal-checklist.md`, `typescript-checklist.md`, `python-checklist.md`, `shell-checklist.md`, `javascript-checklist.md`, `rust-checklist/` (split into topic parts), `config-checklist.md`\n158: \n159: Verifier assets (`assets/scripts/`): alignment-drift and stack-folder verifier scripts, the router-sync guard (`verify-router-sync.cjs`) with its replay library (`router-replay-lib.cjs`) and the documentation claim guard (`verify-doc-claims.cjs`), used by this surface. `scripts/run-all-drift-guards.sh` is the single entry point that runs all four as one gate (non-zero if any fails).\n160: \n161: Changelog directories (`changelog/`): real skill and packet changelog files are part of the Skilled surface inventory, not out-of-scope documentation noise.\n162: \n163: Daemon IPC/socket wiring: daemon-backed CLIs and plugin bridges resolve runtime Unix socket paths such as `daemon-ipc.sock`; sockets are runtime artifacts, so checked-in evidence lives in launcher, bridge, and config files rather than in committed `.sock` files.\n164: \n165: Spec-folder authoring lives in system-spec-kit (`.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` + `spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md`), not in this surface. Checklists are pulled on demand by the active workflow phase.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 6. QUALITY CHECKLIST MAP\n170: \n171: This is this repository's quality checklist map, not a universal one.\n172: The quality runner resolves checklists from two sources: the rows owned by the quality mode in `../sk-code-quality/assets/quality-defaults.json` and each surface's `quality.checklists` declaration in `../mode-registry.json`.\n173: This table renders both sources in that order.\n174: `scripts/tests/quality-checklist-map.test.cjs` fails when the two differ.\n175: In any repository, run `node ../sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` to see the checklists for a file and why they apply.\n176: \n177: A SKILLED row applies only to a file that resolves to SKILLED.\n178: A `sk-code-ci` row applies by path alone because that surface declares no detection block.\n179: The spec-folder row applies only inside this hub's own repository.\n180: Applies to lists path globs and extensions. A row with several values matches on any of them.\n181: The MCP server checklist, `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`, has no path to declare, so load it by judgment when your target is MCP server source.\n182: \n183: | Owner | Applies to | Checklist |\n184: | --- | --- | --- |\n185: | quality defaults | `**` | `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` |\n186: | quality defaults | `specs/**` | `../../system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` |\n187: | SKILLED | `.skilled/**` | `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` |\n188: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/**` | `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n189: | SKILLED | `.skilled/agents/**` | `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n190: | SKILLED | `.skilled/commands/**` | `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n191: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.cjs`, `.mjs` | `assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n192: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md` |\n193: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md` |\n194: | SKILLED | `.js`, `.css` | `assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md` |\n195: | SKILLED | `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.mts`, `.cts` | `assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n196: | SKILLED | `.py` | `assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n197: | SKILLED | `.sh`, `.bash` | `assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n198: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md` |\n199: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md` |\n200: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md` |\n201: | SKILLED | `.rs` | `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md` |\n202: | SKILLED | `.json`, `.jsonc`, `.yaml`, `.yml` | `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n203: | sk-code-ci | `.github/workflows/**` | `../sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589350293,"end":1794589351135}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589351141,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc5ba3001odiH0tOOH5HuTU","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc4d1c001zMK4MiRuVbyAcf","type":"tool","id":"call_bf0ac88b8cc14f05a9dfae7b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/ROUTER.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/ROUTER.md, lines 1-171\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence Router\"\n3: description: \"Sibling router for sk-code-skilled: the intent signals, the resource map and the no-match checklist a reader parses first.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code-skilled router\"\n6:   - \"sk-code-skilled routing\"\n7: importance_tier: normal\n8: contextType: general\n9: version: 1.0.2.0\n10: router_state: active\n11: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence Router\n15: \n16: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the\n17: link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: The hub bundles this surface when a request touches `.skilled/` system code, such as a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config. A language or authoring signal then selects the leaf, and the first leaves loaded are the shared tier under `references/shared/`. From there the router selects among four families: language references, shared patterns, the authoring and quality checklists under `assets/checklists/`, and the verifier assets under `assets/scripts/`.\n24: \n25: The router answers one question: given a request, which skill-local resources load, in what order, and\n26: what happens when nothing matches. Every path below stays inside this skill folder, and no resource other than `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`\n27: loads before an intent selects it.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n32: \n33: - **IMPLEMENTATION** — \"implement / build / create / feature / component / module / authoring\" requests load the five `references/shared/` pattern and organization docs, then `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`, `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`, `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` and `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`.\n34: - **CODE_QUALITY** — \"lint / format / quality gate / naming / standards / code smell\" requests load `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`, the three `assets/checklists/js-css-style/` parts and the per-language checklists for JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, shell and the four `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` parts.\n35: - **VERIFICATION** — \"verify / passing / type-check / alignment drift / completion claim\" requests load `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md` and `assets/scripts/README.md`.\n36: - **HOOKS** — \"session-prime / user-prompt-submit / pre-tool-use / post-tool-use\" requests load `references/shared/hooks.md` and nothing else, so hook wiring stays in one file.\n37: - **CONFIG** — \"jsonc / .json / .jsonc / descriptor / config schema\" requests load `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md` and `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`.\n38: - **JAVASCRIPT** — \"javascript / .js / commonjs / .cjs / .mjs\" requests load `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, both `references/javascript/quality-standards/` parts and `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`.\n39: - **TYPESCRIPT** — \"typescript / .ts / .tsx\" requests load the seven `references/typescript/` docs: two style-guide parts, three quality-standards parts and two quick-reference parts.\n40: - **PYTHON** — \"python / .py / docstring\" requests load `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md` and `references/python/quick-reference.md`.\n41: - **SHELL** — \"shell script / bash / .sh\" requests load the six `references/shell/` docs: two style-guide parts, two quality-standards parts and two quick-reference parts.\n42: - **RUST** — \"rust / .rs / cargo.toml / cargo.lock / napi-rs / napi_rs / #[napi] / wasm-bindgen / wasm_bindgen / #[wasm_bindgen] / wasi / cdylib\" requests load the seventeen `references/rust/` docs: seven style-guide parts, five quality-standards parts and five quick-reference parts.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n47: \n48: The one machine-readable projection of the intent model above, written as `## MACHINE ROUTER` so every\n49: reader finds it. This section is the last in the file, and it holds the only pseudocode.\n50: \n51: This block is the deterministic projection of sk-code-skilled's own reference/asset routing; keep it in sync with the parent hub union.\n52: \n53: ```python\n54: # sk-code-skilled owns its intent -> reference/asset routing. Paths are relative to\n55: # this skill root. The parent sk-code hub RESOURCE_MAP is the union of this map\n56: # (re-prefixed with sk-code-skilled/) plus the\n57: # parent-owned universal/shared tier. The router-sync guard\n58: # (assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs, leg 2) checks that equality.\n59: # verify_alignment_drift.py --check-router checks dead routes only, so it is not\n60: # the equality authority. Leg 1b of the same guard fails on any reference or asset doc that\n61: # no router names, so a new doc under references/ or assets/ needs a RESOURCE_MAP entry here.\n62: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n63:     \"references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n64:     \"references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n65:     \"references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n66:     \"references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n67:     \"references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n68:     \"references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md\",\n69: ]\n70: \n71: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n72:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n73:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\"]},\n74:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n75:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n76:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n77:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n78:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n79:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n80:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n81:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n82: }\n83: \n84: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n85:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n86:         \"references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n87:         \"references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n88:         \"references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n89:         \"references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n90:         \"references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n91:         \"assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n92:         \"assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n93:         \"assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n94:         \"assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n95:     ],\n96:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n97:         \"assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",\n98:         \"assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n99:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n100:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n101:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n102:         \"assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n103:         \"assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n104:         \"assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n105:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n106:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n107:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n108:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n109:     ],\n110:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n111:         \"references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n112:         \"assets/scripts/README.md\",\n113:     ],\n114:     \"HOOKS\": [\n115:         \"references/shared/hooks.md\",\n116:     ],\n117:     \"CONFIG\": [\n118:         \"references/config/style-guide.md\",\n119:         \"references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n120:         \"references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n121:         \"assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n122:     ],\n123:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n124:         \"references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n125:         \"references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n126:         \"references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n127:         \"references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n128:     ],\n129:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n130:         \"references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n131:         \"references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n132:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n133:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n134:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n135:         \"references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n136:         \"references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n137:     ],\n138:     \"PYTHON\": [\n139:         \"references/python/style-guide.md\",\n140:         \"references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n141:         \"references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n142:     ],\n143:     \"SHELL\": [\n144:         \"references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n145:         \"references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n146:         \"references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n147:         \"references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n148:         \"references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n149:         \"references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n150:     ],\n151:     \"RUST\": [\n152:         \"references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n153:         \"references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n154:         \"references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n155:         \"references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n156:         \"references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n157:         \"references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n158:         \"references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n159:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n160:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n161:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n162:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n163:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n164:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n165:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n166:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n167:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n168:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n169:     ],\n170: }\n171: ```","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/ROUTER.md, lines 1-171\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence Router\"\n3: description: \"Sibling router for sk-code-skilled: the intent signals, the resource map and the no-match checklist a reader parses first.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code-skilled router\"\n6:   - \"sk-code-skilled routing\"\n7: importance_tier: normal\n8: contextType: general\n9: version: 1.0.2.0\n10: router_state: active\n11: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Skilled Surface — System-Code Evidence Router\n15: \n16: The machine router for [`SKILL.md`](./SKILL.md). `SKILL.md` keeps the short routing prose and the\n17: link that points here; this file holds the declaration a reader parses to decide what loads.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: The hub bundles this surface when a request touches `.skilled/` system code, such as a skill, agent, command, plugin, MCP server, descriptor or config. A language or authoring signal then selects the leaf, and the first leaves loaded are the shared tier under `references/shared/`. From there the router selects among four families: language references, shared patterns, the authoring and quality checklists under `assets/checklists/`, and the verifier assets under `assets/scripts/`.\n24: \n25: The router answers one question: given a request, which skill-local resources load, in what order, and\n26: what happens when nothing matches. Every path below stays inside this skill folder, and no resource other than `DEFAULT_RESOURCE`\n27: loads before an intent selects it.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n32: \n33: - **IMPLEMENTATION** — \"implement / build / create / feature / component / module / authoring\" requests load the five `references/shared/` pattern and organization docs, then `assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md`, `assets/checklists/command-authoring.md`, `assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` and `assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md`.\n34: - **CODE_QUALITY** — \"lint / format / quality gate / naming / standards / code smell\" requests load `assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md`, the three `assets/checklists/js-css-style/` parts and the per-language checklists for JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, shell and the four `assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` parts.\n35: - **VERIFICATION** — \"verify / passing / type-check / alignment drift / completion claim\" requests load `references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md` and `assets/scripts/README.md`.\n36: - **HOOKS** — \"session-prime / user-prompt-submit / pre-tool-use / post-tool-use\" requests load `references/shared/hooks.md` and nothing else, so hook wiring stays in one file.\n37: - **CONFIG** — \"jsonc / .json / .jsonc / descriptor / config schema\" requests load `references/config/style-guide.md`, `references/config/quality-standards.md`, `references/config/quick-reference.md` and `assets/checklists/config-checklist.md`.\n38: - **JAVASCRIPT** — \"javascript / .js / commonjs / .cjs / .mjs\" requests load `references/javascript/style-guide.md`, both `references/javascript/quality-standards/` parts and `references/javascript/quick-reference.md`.\n39: - **TYPESCRIPT** — \"typescript / .ts / .tsx\" requests load the seven `references/typescript/` docs: two style-guide parts, three quality-standards parts and two quick-reference parts.\n40: - **PYTHON** — \"python / .py / docstring\" requests load `references/python/style-guide.md`, `references/python/quality-standards.md` and `references/python/quick-reference.md`.\n41: - **SHELL** — \"shell script / bash / .sh\" requests load the six `references/shell/` docs: two style-guide parts, two quality-standards parts and two quick-reference parts.\n42: - **RUST** — \"rust / .rs / cargo.toml / cargo.lock / napi-rs / napi_rs / #[napi] / wasm-bindgen / wasm_bindgen / #[wasm_bindgen] / wasi / cdylib\" requests load the seventeen `references/rust/` docs: seven style-guide parts, five quality-standards parts and five quick-reference parts.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 3. MACHINE ROUTER\n47: \n48: The one machine-readable projection of the intent model above, written as `## MACHINE ROUTER` so every\n49: reader finds it. This section is the last in the file, and it holds the only pseudocode.\n50: \n51: This block is the deterministic projection of sk-code-skilled's own reference/asset routing; keep it in sync with the parent hub union.\n52: \n53: ```python\n54: # sk-code-skilled owns its intent -> reference/asset routing. Paths are relative to\n55: # this skill root. The parent sk-code hub RESOURCE_MAP is the union of this map\n56: # (re-prefixed with sk-code-skilled/) plus the\n57: # parent-owned universal/shared tier. The router-sync guard\n58: # (assets/scripts/verify-router-sync.cjs, leg 2) checks that equality.\n59: # verify_alignment_drift.py --check-router checks dead routes only, so it is not\n60: # the equality authority. Leg 1b of the same guard fails on any reference or asset doc that\n61: # no router names, so a new doc under references/ or assets/ needs a RESOURCE_MAP entry here.\n62: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n63:     \"references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n64:     \"references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n65:     \"references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n66:     \"references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n67:     \"references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n68:     \"references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md\",\n69: ]\n70: \n71: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n72:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n73:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\"]},\n74:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n75:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n76:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n77:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n78:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n79:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n80:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n81:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n82: }\n83: \n84: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n85:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n86:         \"references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n87:         \"references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n88:         \"references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n89:         \"references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n90:         \"references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n91:         \"assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n92:         \"assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n93:         \"assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n94:         \"assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n95:     ],\n96:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n97:         \"assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",\n98:         \"assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n99:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n100:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n101:         \"assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n102:         \"assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n103:         \"assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n104:         \"assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n105:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n106:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n107:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n108:         \"assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n109:     ],\n110:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n111:         \"references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n112:         \"assets/scripts/README.md\",\n113:     ],\n114:     \"HOOKS\": [\n115:         \"references/shared/hooks.md\",\n116:     ],\n117:     \"CONFIG\": [\n118:         \"references/config/style-guide.md\",\n119:         \"references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n120:         \"references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n121:         \"assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n122:     ],\n123:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n124:         \"references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n125:         \"references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n126:         \"references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n127:         \"references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n128:     ],\n129:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n130:         \"references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n131:         \"references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n132:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n133:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n134:         \"references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n135:         \"references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n136:         \"references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n137:     ],\n138:     \"PYTHON\": [\n139:         \"references/python/style-guide.md\",\n140:         \"references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n141:         \"references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n142:     ],\n143:     \"SHELL\": [\n144:         \"references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n145:         \"references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n146:         \"references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n147:         \"references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n148:         \"references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n149:         \"references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n150:     ],\n151:     \"RUST\": [\n152:         \"references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n153:         \"references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n154:         \"references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n155:         \"references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n156:         \"references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n157:         \"references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n158:         \"references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n159:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n160:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n161:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n162:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n163:         \"references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n164:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n165:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n166:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n167:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n168:         \"references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n169:     ],\n170: }\n171: ```"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/ROUTER.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589350819,"end":1794589351141}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794589355257,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"id":"prt_1d5dc6cf9001Y66Mgl93QLFwHk","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc4d1c001zMK4MiRuVbyAcf","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":521,"output":142,"reasoning":68,"cache":{"read":73344,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794589357781,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"id":"prt_1d5dc76d5001qNQxy6ssPtUQQ4","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc6cff001hypTGSECbkJ4yz","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589362982,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc87bb001ukBadK5WOCy0a4","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc6cff001hypTGSECbkJ4yz","type":"tool","id":"call_1e731543890c46c88a395b06","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigg