\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589362107,"end":1794589362982}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589363015,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc898e001sbsB0OrdlKdqhy","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc6cff001hypTGSECbkJ4yz","type":"tool","id":"call_010226799e2f4d90a8061f8b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589362574,"end":1794589363015}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589363063,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc85a80011qmqfmAbieIGT9","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc6cff001hypTGSECbkJ4yz","type":"tool","id":"call_60aadfcaef6b4c6fa52a897c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md, lines 1-177\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Verification\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n6:   - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"falsifier discipline\"\n8:   - \"completion claim gate\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.2.7.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Verification\n15: \n16: Open this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Verification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n29: - Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n30: - Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n31: - Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n32: - Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n33: - Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n34: \n35: ### When Not to Use\n36: \n37: - The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n38: - The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n39: - Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n40: - The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n41: - The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n42: \n43: ### Iron Law\n44: \n45: **Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n46: \n47: A clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n52: \n53: ### Verification Loop\n54: \n55: 1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n56: 2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n57:    - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n58:    - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n59:    - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n60:    - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n61:    - The run verdict has three readers: this step, the @code RETURN `Quality Gate` field and `sk-code-review`, which reads it as input evidence only.\n62: 3. Name the claim to prove and the command, action or observation that proves it.\n63: 4. Select the surface-appropriate command set and targeted tests from the active-surface guidance.\n64: 5. Run fresh commands and capture the command names, exit codes, test counts, important output and failures.\n65: 6. Add end-to-end, runtime or performance evidence when the claim depends on behavior outside static checks.\n66: 7. Compare the current evidence to the baseline and report the delta.\n67: 8. Run a mutation check or claim falsifier when the claim depends on a new or changed test.\n68: 9. Walk the Pre-Claim Gate below, then state only the claims the evidence proves. If the evidence fails, hand back without editing.\n69: \n70: ### Baseline And Delta\n71: \n72: Verification compares against the baseline whenever it states a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n73: \n74: | Field | Required Content |\n75: | --- | --- |\n76: | Baseline | Starting command status, failing tests, warning count, runtime issue, performance number or `UNKNOWN` when no baseline was captured. |\n77: | Current | Fresh output from the whole gate, not only the subset you iterated on: exit codes, test counts, runtime observations or measured numbers. |\n78: | Delta | What changed, improved, regressed or remains unproven, including the parts that did not move. |\n79: | Claim Scope | The exact claim the evidence supports and the blind spot that remains. |\n80: \n81: No baseline means no broad no-regressions claim. A narrower claim tied to fresh evidence still stands.\n82: \n83: The baseline floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 5 (BASELINES). This table is the sk-code record shape for it. Implementation records its Baseline row before the first edit.\n84: \n85: ### Verification Ladder\n86: \n87: Climb from the cheapest rung to the most authoritative and name what each rung cannot see:\n88: \n89: | Rung | Proves | Blind Spot |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | Unit | Isolated logic or assertion path | Integration, wiring, real I/O, runtime state |\n92: | In-memory | Multiple modules without external runtime | Real file system, serialization, deployed config, external state |\n93: | On-server | Runtime service or local server path | Deployment differences, caches, real user environment |\n94: | Live | Exact exercised path in real target | Unexercised paths, other users, other environments, future data |\n95: \n96: ### Mutation Check And Claim Falsifier\n97: \n98: After green evidence for a new or modified test, confirm the test can fail for the right reason when that is feasible. A true red result is an assertion failing against the intended behavior. A compile error, or a command that never reached the assertion, is not a satisfying red. If a test stays green after the guarded behavior is broken, report it as a verification defect and hand back.\n99: \n100: This applies the idea behind `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 4 (THE NEGATIVE CONTROL): a check that passes whether or not the behavior works proves nothing. The before-fix half of that control, reproducing the failure with the same check, is step 2 of the [Debug Loop](./workflow-debug.md#debug-loop).\n101: \n102: ### Pre-Claim Gate Substance\n103: \n104: Walk every gate in order before any status claim:\n105: \n106: | Gate | Required Discipline |\n107: | --- | --- |\n108: | Identify | Name the command, action or observation that proves the claim. |\n109: | Run | Execute the surface-appropriate verification commands in the current session. |\n110: | Test | Exercise targeted unit, integration, end-to-end, runtime or manual checks where available, including at least one edge case and one error path. |\n111: | Verify | Confirm the output, exit code, test count and observed behavior match expectations, and that the run reached the changed code on a fresh build with real assertions. |\n112: | Record | Save the exact evidence the final claim rests on. |\n113: | Reread | Reread the request and its amendments, and confirm each outcome it asks for is backed by recorded evidence or named as not done. |\n114: | Claim | State only the narrow claim the evidence supports. |\n115: \n116: The Verify row guards against the four ways a green run lies, which `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 3 lists: it did not run, it ran on the wrong thing, it ran on stale artifacts or it asserted nothing.\n117: \n118: ### OpenCode Verification Reality\n119: \n120: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [verification reality](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#3-verification-reality), including the `validate.sh` pointer, and the [runtime build traps](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#4-runtime-build-traps).\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n125: \n126: ### Always\n127: \n128: - Always run fresh verification in the current session before a completion-related claim.\n129: - Always resolve surface identity before selecting commands.\n130: - Always record command names, exit codes, key output, test counts and runtime observations.\n131: - Always report the baseline, current result, delta and claim scope for a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n132: - Always name the verification ladder rung reached and the blind spot that remains.\n133: - Always include behavior evidence when runtime behavior changed and tooling is available.\n134: - Always hand back when verification finds a defect.\n135: - Always confirm the diff scope matches the stated change, with no accidental extras or debug residue.\n136: - Always compare warnings against the last working state, so a new warning is reported and not absorbed.\n137: \n138: ### Never\n139: \n140: - Never edit or create files during verification.\n141: - Never dispatch subagents from verification.\n142: - Never claim done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready from unrun commands.\n143: - Never claim no regressions without a baseline and a delta.\n144: - Never treat one green rung as proof of a higher rung.\n145: - Never hide a failed or skipped verifier behind a positive summary.\n146: - Never invent runtime, test or performance evidence.\n147: - Never verify against stale build output. Clean and rebuild first when an artifact is suspect.\n148: - Never present a check run in one environment as proof for every environment.\n149: \n150: ### Escalate If\n151: \n152: - The surface or the required verification command set is unknown.\n153: - A required verifier path is missing or unsafe to run.\n154: - Commands contradict each other on a blocking outcome.\n155: - Runtime evidence is required but unavailable and the claim would mislead without it.\n156: - The baseline is missing and the requested claim needs a regression comparison.\n157: - A mutation check shows the test is vacuous.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n162: \n163: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives reproducible verification failures, failed commands, broken runtime checks or vacuous tests.\n164: - [Implementation](./workflow-implement.md) receives the handback when required behavior is missing or build work is incomplete.\n165: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the handback when author-side checks, comment hygiene or checklist issues block verification.\n166: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns critique when the user wants findings instead of final evidence.\n167: \n168: Verification is ready to report only when it ran last, used only non-mutating actions, recorded fresh active-surface evidence, compared against the baseline when the claim needed it, named its blind spots and handed back any defect without editing.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n173: \n174: - [workflow-implement.md](./workflow-implement.md) - the build phase whose baseline and handoff this phase reads.\n175: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that receives every failed verification.\n176: - [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - surface detection, which decides the command set.\n177: - [workflow-guardrails.md](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md) - the verification command chain for system code under `.skilled/`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md, lines 1-177\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Verification\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n6:   - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"falsifier discipline\"\n8:   - \"completion claim gate\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.2.7.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Verification\n15: \n16: Open this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Verification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n29: - Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n30: - Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n31: - Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n32: - Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n33: - Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n34: \n35: ### When Not to Use\n36: \n37: - The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n38: - The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n39: - Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n40: - The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n41: - The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n42: \n43: ### Iron Law\n44: \n45: **Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n46: \n47: A clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n52: \n53: ### Verification Loop\n54: \n55: 1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n56: 2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n57:    - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n58:    - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n59:    - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n60:    - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n61:    - The run verdict has three readers: this step, the @code RETURN `Quality Gate` field and `sk-code-review`, which reads it as input evidence only.\n62: 3. Name the claim to prove and the command, action or observation that proves it.\n63: 4. Select the surface-appropriate command set and targeted tests from the active-surface guidance.\n64: 5. Run fresh commands and capture the command names, exit codes, test counts, important output and failures.\n65: 6. Add end-to-end, runtime or performance evidence when the claim depends on behavior outside static checks.\n66: 7. Compare the current evidence to the baseline and report the delta.\n67: 8. Run a mutation check or claim falsifier when the claim depends on a new or changed test.\n68: 9. Walk the Pre-Claim Gate below, then state only the claims the evidence proves. If the evidence fails, hand back without editing.\n69: \n70: ### Baseline And Delta\n71: \n72: Verification compares against the baseline whenever it states a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n73: \n74: | Field | Required Content |\n75: | --- | --- |\n76: | Baseline | Starting command status, failing tests, warning count, runtime issue, performance number or `UNKNOWN` when no baseline was captured. |\n77: | Current | Fresh output from the whole gate, not only the subset you iterated on: exit codes, test counts, runtime observations or measured numbers. |\n78: | Delta | What changed, improved, regressed or remains unproven, including the parts that did not move. |\n79: | Claim Scope | The exact claim the evidence supports and the blind spot that remains. |\n80: \n81: No baseline means no broad no-regressions claim. A narrower claim tied to fresh evidence still stands.\n82: \n83: The baseline floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 5 (BASELINES). This table is the sk-code record shape for it. Implementation records its Baseline row before the first edit.\n84: \n85: ### Verification Ladder\n86: \n87: Climb from the cheapest rung to the most authoritative and name what each rung cannot see:\n88: \n89: | Rung | Proves | Blind Spot |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | Unit | Isolated logic or assertion path | Integration, wiring, real I/O, runtime state |\n92: | In-memory | Multiple modules without external runtime | Real file system, serialization, deployed config, external state |\n93: | On-server | Runtime service or local server path | Deployment differences, caches, real user environment |\n94: | Live | Exact exercised path in real target | Unexercised paths, other users, other environments, future data |\n95: \n96: ### Mutation Check And Claim Falsifier\n97: \n98: After green evidence for a new or modified test, confirm the test can fail for the right reason when that is feasible. A true red result is an assertion failing against the intended behavior. A compile error, or a command that never reached the assertion, is not a satisfying red. If a test stays green after the guarded behavior is broken, report it as a verification defect and hand back.\n99: \n100: This applies the idea behind `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 4 (THE NEGATIVE CONTROL): a check that passes whether or not the behavior works proves nothing. The before-fix half of that control, reproducing the failure with the same check, is step 2 of the [Debug Loop](./workflow-debug.md#debug-loop).\n101: \n102: ### Pre-Claim Gate Substance\n103: \n104: Walk every gate in order before any status claim:\n105: \n106: | Gate | Required Discipline |\n107: | --- | --- |\n108: | Identify | Name the command, action or observation that proves the claim. |\n109: | Run | Execute the surface-appropriate verification commands in the current session. |\n110: | Test | Exercise targeted unit, integration, end-to-end, runtime or manual checks where available, including at least one edge case and one error path. |\n111: | Verify | Confirm the output, exit code, test count and observed behavior match expectations, and that the run reached the changed code on a fresh build with real assertions. |\n112: | Record | Save the exact evidence the final claim rests on. |\n113: | Reread | Reread the request and its amendments, and confirm each outcome it asks for is backed by recorded evidence or named as not done. |\n114: | Claim | State only the narrow claim the evidence supports. |\n115: \n116: The Verify row guards against the four ways a green run lies, which `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 3 lists: it did not run, it ran on the wrong thing, it ran on stale artifacts or it asserted nothing.\n117: \n118: ### OpenCode Verification Reality\n119: \n120: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [verification reality](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#3-verification-reality), including the `validate.sh` pointer, and the [runtime build traps](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#4-runtime-build-traps).\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n125: \n126: ### Always\n127: \n128: - Always run fresh verification in the current session before a completion-related claim.\n129: - Always resolve surface identity before selecting commands.\n130: - Always record command names, exit codes, key output, test counts and runtime observations.\n131: - Always report the baseline, current result, delta and claim scope for a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n132: - Always name the verification ladder rung reached and the blind spot that remains.\n133: - Always include behavior evidence when runtime behavior changed and tooling is available.\n134: - Always hand back when verification finds a defect.\n135: - Always confirm the diff scope matches the stated change, with no accidental extras or debug residue.\n136: - Always compare warnings against the last working state, so a new warning is reported and not absorbed.\n137: \n138: ### Never\n139: \n140: - Never edit or create files during verification.\n141: - Never dispatch subagents from verification.\n142: - Never claim done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready from unrun commands.\n143: - Never claim no regressions without a baseline and a delta.\n144: - Never treat one green rung as proof of a higher rung.\n145: - Never hide a failed or skipped verifier behind a positive summary.\n146: - Never invent runtime, test or performance evidence.\n147: - Never verify against stale build output. Clean and rebuild first when an artifact is suspect.\n148: - Never present a check run in one environment as proof for every environment.\n149: \n150: ### Escalate If\n151: \n152: - The surface or the required verification command set is unknown.\n153: - A required verifier path is missing or unsafe to run.\n154: - Commands contradict each other on a blocking outcome.\n155: - Runtime evidence is required but unavailable and the claim would mislead without it.\n156: - The baseline is missing and the requested claim needs a regression comparison.\n157: - A mutation check shows the test is vacuous.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n162: \n163: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives reproducible verification failures, failed commands, broken runtime checks or vacuous tests.\n164: - [Implementation](./workflow-implement.md) receives the handback when required behavior is missing or build work is incomplete.\n165: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the handback when author-side checks, comment hygiene or checklist issues block verification.\n166: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns critique when the user wants findings instead of final evidence.\n167: \n168: Verification is ready to report only when it ran last, used only non-mutating actions, recorded fresh active-surface evidence, compared against the baseline when the claim needed it, named its blind spots and handed back any defect without editing.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n173: \n174: - [workflow-implement.md](./workflow-implement.md) - the build phase whose baseline and handoff this phase reads.\n175: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that receives every failed verification.\n176: - [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - surface detection, which decides the command set.\n177: - [workflow-guardrails.md](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md) - the verification command chain for system code under `.skilled/`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589361576,"end":1794589363063}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589363112,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dc83c8001YuKiam41FaZ8EM","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dc6cff001hypTGSECbkJ4yz","type":"tool","id":"call_dcb9de3184b84df7a83a0de7","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589361096,"end":1794589363112}}}}
