{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794589372320,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"id":"prt_1d5dcafa0001PuZpoFvlpeSaAJ","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dca0b8001t1uBQtdJSmalni","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589373800,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dcafa5002xulpaL2vw1gLsP","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dca0b8001t1uBQtdJSmalni","type":"tool","id":"call_1f8b61c25d304ce496b29ee1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md, lines 1-71\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\"\n3: description: \"OpenCode-only rules that sit on top of the shared implement and verify workflow: the continuity writer, the sk-git boundary, the spec validation and typecheck chain, and runtime build traps.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode workflow guardrails\"\n6:   - \"opencode verification reality\"\n7:   - \"runtime build traps\"\n8:   - \"rebuild dist before verifying\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\n15: \n16: OpenCode-only rules for the implement and verify phases.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: The shared [implementation](../workflow-implement.md) and [verification](../workflow-verify.md) workflows apply on every surface. This file adds what holds only for OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`: the implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and the runtime build traps.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Implementing or verifying a change to OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`\n29: - Deciding whether a source edit needs a `dist/` rebuild before its behavior is verified\n30: - Handing missing build output, stale generated runtime files or a wrong package boundary back to implementation\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. IMPLEMENTATION GUARDRAILS\n35: \n36: - Treat `generate-context.js` as the single writer for a packet's continuity metadata, invoked through `/speckit:save`. It keeps atomic same-directory update and lock semantics, so do not hand-edit the generated metadata pair alongside it or run a second writer against the same packet. There is no index or embedding store behind it to mutate separately.\n37: - For git worktree isolation, defer to `sk-git`. This workflow may note that isolation is needed, but it must not duplicate `sk-git`'s worktree setup, branch, commit, or finish-work contract.\n38: - Preserve the verification handoff. Implementation should name the package boundary, rebuild requirement, baseline, likely test command, and any env knobs the verifier must pin. Final evidence belongs to [Workflow Reference - Verification](../workflow-verify.md), not implementation.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. VERIFICATION REALITY\n43: \n44: OpenCode verification starts with the real spec validation contract when a spec folder is in scope:\n45: \n46: ```bash\n47: bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The exit-code and warning contract of `validate.sh` is owned by `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md` (section 1 for the exit taxonomy, section 14 for the ways a run misleads). Read it there, not from a copy here. The one fact this workflow needs: a completion claim requires the explicit `RESULT: PASSED` line, because a warning never changes the exit code and exit status alone has misled in both directions.\n51: \n52: Use the package script for the package you changed:\n53: \n54: - **Spec-kit root and project-reference workspaces**: `tsc --build`.\n55: - **Satellite packages with their own package boundary**: `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`. A satellite typecheck script may add `--noEmit --composite false` over that same overlay.\n56: - **TypeScript tests**: run the package's Vitest-backed script where present, such as `npm test`, `npm run test:core`, or the targeted `vitest run ...` command exposed by that package.\n57: \n58: **Which suite a change owes.** Run the full suite of every package the change touches, and only those. A change under `system-spec-kit/runtime` owes `npm test` there. A change under `system-deep-loop/runtime` owes `npm test` there. CI runs the whole matrix after a push, so a local run of a package you did not touch proves nothing new.\n59: \n60: **When a rebase owes a rerun.** List what the rebase brought in with `git diff --stat <old base> <new base>`. When it brought only generated files, such as the trigger index or a packet's `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, the earlier run still stands. When it brought source changes to a package you touched, run that package's suite again.\n61: \n62: When verification discovers missing build output, stale generated runtime files, or a wrong package boundary, hand back to [Workflow Reference - Implementation](../workflow-implement.md) before making any completion claim.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 4. RUNTIME BUILD TRAPS\n67: \n68: - MCP servers, daemon-backed CLIs, and runtime hooks execute built `dist/` output. Editing a `.ts` source file has no runtime effect until the owning package rebuilds its `dist/` artifacts.\n69: - Rebuild before verifying behavior that depends on generated output. For `system-skill-advisor`, the server package names `dist/mcp-server/advisor-server.js` as the compiled backend artifact and `npm run build` as the command that builds TypeScript into `dist/`.\n70: - Keep env-sensitive tests deterministic. Set feature flags, provider choices, database paths, and timeout knobs explicitly in the command or test fixture. Record those values with the result. Do not rely on inherited shell state when the claim depends on a flag.\n71: - After a Node version change, run the native rebuild helper from the spec-kit root: `bash scripts/setup/rebuild-native-modules.sh`. It rebuilds native modules including `better-sqlite3` in `runtime/` and shared workspace modules, then records the new Node version marker.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md, lines 1-71\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\"\n3: description: \"OpenCode-only rules that sit on top of the shared implement and verify workflow: the continuity writer, the sk-git boundary, the spec validation and typecheck chain, and runtime build traps.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode workflow guardrails\"\n6:   - \"opencode verification reality\"\n7:   - \"runtime build traps\"\n8:   - \"rebuild dist before verifying\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\n15: \n16: OpenCode-only rules for the implement and verify phases.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: The shared [implementation](../workflow-implement.md) and [verification](../workflow-verify.md) workflows apply on every surface. This file adds what holds only for OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`: the implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and the runtime build traps.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Implementing or verifying a change to OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`\n29: - Deciding whether a source edit needs a `dist/` rebuild before its behavior is verified\n30: - Handing missing build output, stale generated runtime files or a wrong package boundary back to implementation\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. IMPLEMENTATION GUARDRAILS\n35: \n36: - Treat `generate-context.js` as the single writer for a packet's continuity metadata, invoked through `/speckit:save`. It keeps atomic same-directory update and lock semantics, so do not hand-edit the generated metadata pair alongside it or run a second writer against the same packet. There is no index or embedding store behind it to mutate separately.\n37: - For git worktree isolation, defer to `sk-git`. This workflow may note that isolation is needed, but it must not duplicate `sk-git`'s worktree setup, branch, commit, or finish-work contract.\n38: - Preserve the verification handoff. Implementation should name the package boundary, rebuild requirement, baseline, likely test command, and any env knobs the verifier must pin. Final evidence belongs to [Workflow Reference - Verification](../workflow-verify.md), not implementation.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. VERIFICATION REALITY\n43: \n44: OpenCode verification starts with the real spec validation contract when a spec folder is in scope:\n45: \n46: ```bash\n47: bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The exit-code and warning contract of `validate.sh` is owned by `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md` (section 1 for the exit taxonomy, section 14 for the ways a run misleads). Read it there, not from a copy here. The one fact this workflow needs: a completion claim requires the explicit `RESULT: PASSED` line, because a warning never changes the exit code and exit status alone has misled in both directions.\n51: \n52: Use the package script for the package you changed:\n53: \n54: - **Spec-kit root and project-reference workspaces**: `tsc --build`.\n55: - **Satellite packages with their own package boundary**: `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`. A satellite typecheck script may add `--noEmit --composite false` over that same overlay.\n56: - **TypeScript tests**: run the package's Vitest-backed script where present, such as `npm test`, `npm run test:core`, or the targeted `vitest run ...` command exposed by that package.\n57: \n58: **Which suite a change owes.** Run the full suite of every package the change touches, and only those. A change under `system-spec-kit/runtime` owes `npm test` there. A change under `system-deep-loop/runtime` owes `npm test` there. CI runs the whole matrix after a push, so a local run of a package you did not touch proves nothing new.\n59: \n60: **When a rebase owes a rerun.** List what the rebase brought in with `git diff --stat <old base> <new base>`. When it brought only generated files, such as the trigger index or a packet's `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, the earlier run still stands. When it brought source changes to a package you touched, run that package's suite again.\n61: \n62: When verification discovers missing build output, stale generated runtime files, or a wrong package boundary, hand back to [Workflow Reference - Implementation](../workflow-implement.md) before making any completion claim.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 4. RUNTIME BUILD TRAPS\n67: \n68: - MCP servers, daemon-backed CLIs, and runtime hooks execute built `dist/` output. Editing a `.ts` source file has no runtime effect until the owning package rebuilds its `dist/` artifacts.\n69: - Rebuild before verifying behavior that depends on generated output. For `system-skill-advisor`, the server package names `dist/mcp-server/advisor-server.js` as the compiled backend artifact and `npm run build` as the command that builds TypeScript into `dist/`.\n70: - Keep env-sensitive tests deterministic. Set feature flags, provider choices, database paths, and timeout knobs explicitly in the command or test fixture. Record those values with the result. Do not rely on inherited shell state when the claim depends on a flag.\n71: - After a Node version change, run the native rebuild helper from the spec-kit root: `bash scripts/setup/rebuild-native-modules.sh`. It rebuilds native modules including `better-sqlite3` in `runtime/` and shared workspace modules, then records the new Node version marker."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589372325,"end":1794589373800}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589373821,"sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dcb426001NrQwDJSbaL7fRe","sessionID":"ses_e2a256410ffejPK5IfFGf0oN97","messageID":"msg_1d5dca0b8001t1uBQtdJSmalni","type":"tool","id":"call_4c32aa68c6ea4973aa8990a1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\